{#
	One module's page: what it is, what it can be installed as, and what it needs
	(XIV-6).

	Everything on it is read off the blueprint, so a module added to a future
	build gets this page with nothing written here about it.

	The presets are laid out in full rather than summarised, and that is the point
	of the page rather than a nicety: §6.1 does not retro-fit, and XIV-70 does not
	exist yet, so the field list somebody reads here is the field list they live
	with. The wizard says it again where the choice is actually made.
#}
{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}{{ offer.label }}{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
	{{ include('_topbar.html.twig') }}

	<main class="container page-shell">
		{{ include('_flashes.html.twig') }}

		<div class="d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between mb-3">
			<h1 class="h3 mb-0 d-flex align-items-center gap-2">
				{% if offer.blueprint.icon %}
					<i class="bi bi-{{ offer.blueprint.icon }} text-body-secondary" aria-hidden="true"></i>
				{% endif %}
				{{ offer.label }}
			</h1>
			<a class="btn btn-link" href="{{ path('store_index') }}">{{ 'common.back'|trans }}</a>
		</div>

		{% if offer.installed %}
			<div class="alert alert-success">
				{{ 'store.already_yours'|trans }}
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			**What it costs, said once and plainly** (XIV-102).

			Nothing is drawn at all for a free module, so this page is byte-identical
			to what it was before the ticket for every module in this build — see
			`_price.html.twig` for why silence is the right presentation of free.

			For a priced one it says the figure, and then it says what pressing the
			button will and will not do, *above* the button rather than under it. That
			ordering is §6.3's rule about the preset warning borrowed for a smaller
			decision: a sentence somebody needs before they act belongs before the
			control, not as a caption for something they have already clicked.

			**A module that became priced after it was installed keeps working**, and
			this page says nothing about it: the "already yours" alert is what a
			customer in that position sees, no button appears, and there is nothing
			anywhere that treats "priced and installed" as an anomaly to correct. That
			is §6.5's rule — a price says what may be obtained from now on, never what
			is taken away — held on the customer's own screen rather than only in the
			control plane.
		#}
		{% if offer.costsMoney and not offer.installed %}
			<div class="card mb-4">
				<div class="card-body d-flex flex-wrap align-items-baseline gap-3">
					<div>
						<div class="text-body-secondary small">{{ 'store.price'|trans }}</div>
						<div class="h4 mb-0">
							{{ include('store/_price.html.twig', {offer: offer, currency: currency, emphasis: ''}) }}
						</div>
					</div>
					<p class="text-body-secondary small mb-0 flex-grow-1">
						{{ 'store.price_one_off'|trans }}
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			They have already asked. Said here as an answer rather than as a status
			badge, because the question in their head is "why has nothing happened",
			and the honest reply names both halves: it was written down, and a person
			has to act on it.
		#}
		{% if offer.hasBeenRequested %}
			<div class="alert alert-info">
				{{ 'store.requested_on'|trans({'%date%': offer.requested.requestedAt|date('Y-m-d H:i')}) }}
				{{ 'store.requested_next'|trans }}
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			What is missing, named, with a link to it — never chain-installed
			(XIV-23). Each of those modules carries its own permanent preset
			choice, so installing them on somebody's behalf would make a decision
			nothing can undo while they thought they were making one.
		#}
		{% if offer.requirements is not empty %}
			<h2 class="h5 mb-2">{{ 'store.requirements'|trans }}</h2>
			<p class="text-body-secondary">{{ 'store.requirements_help'|trans }}</p>

			<div class="card mb-4">
				<ul class="list-group list-group-flush">
					{% for requirement in offer.requirements %}
						<li class="list-group-item d-flex align-items-center justify-content-between">
							<span>
								{% if requirement.offered %}
									<a href="{{ path('store_module', {module: requirement.key}) }}">{{ requirement.label }}</a>
								{% else %}
									{{ requirement.label }}
								{% endif %}
							</span>
							{% if requirement.installed %}
								<span class="badge text-bg-success">{{ 'store.installed'|trans }}</span>
							{% else %}
								<span class="badge text-bg-warning">{{ 'store.not_installed'|trans }}</span>
							{% endif %}
						</li>
					{% endfor %}
				</ul>
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		<h2 class="h5 mb-2">{{ 'store.presets'|trans }}</h2>
		<p class="text-body-secondary">{{ 'store.presets_help'|trans }}</p>

		{% if offer.presets is empty %}
			<div class="alert alert-secondary">
				{{ 'store.no_presets'|trans({'%count%': offer.blueprint.fields|length}) }}
			</div>
		{% else %}
			<div class="row g-3 mb-4">
				{% for preset in offer.presets %}
					<div class="col-md-6">
						<div class="card h-100">
							<div class="card-body">
								<h3 class="h6 card-title mb-1">
									{{ preset.label }}
									{% if preset.isDefault %}
										<span class="badge text-bg-secondary">{{ 'store.preset_default'|trans }}</span>
									{% endif %}
								</h3>
								<p class="card-text text-body-secondary small">{{ preset.description }}</p>
								{# The fields themselves, not a count. A count is a number
								   somebody has to trust; the list is the decision. #}
								<ul class="small mb-0">
									{% for field in preset.fields %}
										<li>{{ field }}</li>
									{% endfor %}
								</ul>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>
				{% endfor %}
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		{#
			Collections come whatever the preset says, because a preset names fields
			and never collections: a field left out can be added back in the metadata
			editor, and a collection needs a table only the installer creates (§6.1).
			Said here so the shape somebody is choosing is the whole shape.
		#}
		{% if offer.collections is not empty %}
			<div class="alert alert-secondary">
				{{ 'store.collections_always'|trans({'%collections%': offer.collections|join(', ')}) }}
			</div>
		{% endif %}

		<div class="d-flex gap-2">
			{% if offer.isInstallable and can('install', chrome.store) %}
				<a class="btn btn-primary" href="{{ path('store_install', {module: offer.key}) }}">
					<i class="bi bi-download" aria-hidden="true"></i>
					{{ 'store.install'|trans }}
				</a>
			{#
				**The paying branch, and it is a different grant** (XIV-102).
				`can('buy', …)` rather than `can('install', …)`: deciding what this
				installation consists of and committing the company to a payment are
				different authorities, and StoreAction::Buy has the argument. Somebody
				who may install and may not buy sees the price, sees the sentence, and
				sees no button — which is the honest report of where they stand rather
				than a control that 403s.

				`ask` rather than `buy` on the label, because the button does not buy
				anything: it opens a page that explains what will happen and has a
				second button on it. Two steps for a decision about money is the same
				care §6.3 takes over a preset, and the intermediate page is where the
				"nothing is charged" sentence lives.
			#}
			{% elseif offer.isBuyable and not offer.hasBeenRequested and can('buy', chrome.store) %}
				<a class="btn btn-primary" href="{{ path('store_buy', {module: offer.key}) }}">
					<i class="bi bi-bag" aria-hidden="true"></i>
					{{ 'store.ask_to_buy'|trans }}
				</a>
			{% elseif not offer.installed and offer.missingRequirements is not empty %}
				{#
					No button at all rather than a disabled one. A control that cannot
					be used is a question with no answer on it; the sentence beside it
					is the answer, and the links above are the way to fix it.
				#}
				<span class="text-body-secondary">
					{{ 'store.needs_first'|trans({'%missing%': offer.missingRequirements|map(r => r.label)|join(', ')}) }}
				</span>
			{% endif %}
		</div>
	</main>
{% endblock %}
